{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"cornmeal-snively-spreadwing","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe Janitor approval for 038d explicitly authorizes removing eight long-lived env/config aliases instead of keeping them as permanent ergonomic renames. Before code or docs flip those aliases to tombstones, the project needs an ADR that records the approval, names each canonical replacement, explains why these pre-1.0 removals are allowed, and distinguishes internal/runtime compatibility decisions from public stability promises.\n\n## Approach\n\nAllocate the next ADR number through `docs/adr/.numbers`, write the ADR in the standard RESEARCH -> DRAFT -> HOLD -> MERGE lifecycle, and cite the eight removed spellings with their canonical replacements. Cover why `REBAR_NO_SYNC` and `REBAR_ID_GUARD_MODE` are especially unsafe to keep as silent aliases, and state the positive policy for the remaining six inert renames so follow-on implementation can apply the approved removal/tombstone behavior consistently.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `docs/adr/.numbers`\n- new ADR under `docs/adr/`\n- `docs/api-stability.md` if the ADR needs a cross-reference from the public/private stability policy\n\n## Testing\n\nAdd or update the policy/documentation evidence the ADR requires, and prove existing public facade / wire / persisted-data exceptions remain documented as separate contracts.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A merged ADR cites the Janitor approval and records all eight removed spellings with their canonical replacements.\n- [ ] The ADR explains the allowed pre-1.0 removal/tombstone policy for these aliases, including the `REBAR_NO_SYNC` and `REBAR_ID_GUARD_MODE` safety rationale.\n- [ ] The ADR distinguishes public/wire/persisted-data compatibility from internal ergonomic aliases so later migrations do not over-apply the rule.\n- [ ] Any required policy/test/documentation evidence named by the ADR is updated with passing proof.","id":"8ca7-28c5-c5c4-4f32","parent_id":"038d-f5ff-b371-4ba5","priority":2,"tags":["janitor","config","breaking-change","compatibility"],"ticket_type":"task","title":"ADR: retire config aliases"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788421598951045001,"uuid":"ac326d88-3a0a-4ef8-bbd8-8178ad7e3cef"}